## Solution

**Reformulating the condition.** For $S_1 \subsetneq S_2$ in $\mathcal{F}$, we require $|S_2\setminus S_1|$ odd. Since $|S_2\setminus S_1| = |S_2|-|S_1|$, this is equivalent to: $|S_1|$ and $|S_2|$ have **different parity**.

**Consequence.** Split $\mathcal{F} = \mathcal{F}_{\text{even}} \cup \mathcal{F}_{\text{odd}}$ by parity of size. If two sets of the *same* parity were comparable, their size difference would be even — forbidden. Hence:
- $\mathcal{F}_{\text{even}}$ is an **antichain** (among even-sized subsets),
- $\mathcal{F}_{\text{odd}}$ is an **antichain** (among odd-sized subsets),
- there is **no restriction** between an even and an odd member (their difference is automatically odd).

So we maximize $|\mathcal{F}_{\text{even}}| + |\mathcal{F}_{\text{odd}}|$ independently.

**Upper bound via symmetric chain decomposition.** By the de Bruijn–Tengbergen–Kruyswijk theorem, the Boolean lattice $B_{2017}$ admits a symmetric chain decomposition (SCD) into $\binom{2017}{1008}$ chains (the middle rank size, since $n=2017$ is odd, middle ranks $1008, 1009$ both have size $\binom{2017}{1008}$). Each chain runs from rank $k$ to rank $2017-k$ with $k\le 1008$, so **every chain passes through both rank $1008$ (even) and rank $1009$ (odd)**.

Restricting each SCD chain to its even-rank elements gives a chain in the even subposet; since every chain contains a rank-$1008$ element, the even subposet is partitioned into exactly $\binom{2017}{1008}$ chains. By Dilworth's theorem, every antichain in the even subposet has size $\le \binom{2017}{1008}$, achieved by the full rank-$1008$ layer. Thus
$$|\mathcal{F}_{\text{even}}| \le \tbinom{2017}{1008}.$$
Likewise the odd subposet is partitioned into $\binom{2017}{1008}$ chains (each contains a rank-$1009$ element), giving
$$|\mathcal{F}_{\text{odd}}| \le \tbinom{2017}{1009} = \tbinom{2017}{1008}.$$
Therefore
$$|\mathcal{F}| \le \tbinom{2017}{1008} + \tbinom{2017}{1009} = \tbinom{2018}{1009},$$
using Pascal's identity.

**Lower bound (construction).** Take
$$\mathcal{F} = \{S \subseteq [2017] : |S|=1008\} \cup \{S \subseteq [2017] : |S|=1009\}.$$
Two sets of equal size are incomparable. The only possible strict inclusions are $|S_1|=1008,\ |S_2|=1009$ with $S_1\subsetneq S_2$, giving $|S_2\setminus S_1|=1$, which is odd. ✓ The size is $\binom{2017}{1008}+\binom{2017}{1009}=\binom{2018}{1009}$.

**Conclusion.** The largest possible size is

$$\boxed{\dbinom{2018}{1009}}$$

